		{
			"id":"27d9731a31ef9d9b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# fairness-constraint and graph contrastive\n\n\n## 1️⃣ Fairness Constraint in Recommendation Systems\n\n![Image](https://media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs11257-023-09364-z/MediaObjects/11257_2023_9364_Fig12_HTML.png)\n\n![Image](https://miro.medium.com/1%2Afzg_jsAzaUArO84slIuloQ.png)\n\n![Image](https://media.springernature.com/lw1200/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs11257-023-09364-z/MediaObjects/11257_2023_9364_Fig12_HTML.png)\n\n### What it means (plain English)\n\nA **fairness constraint** is a rule added to a recommender system to make sure the recommendations are **not biased or discriminatory** toward certain users or items.\n\nNormally, a recommender just optimizes for accuracy (clicks, watch time, purchases).\nFairness constraints say:\n\n> “Yes, be accurate — **but also be fair**.”\n\n---\n\n### Why fairness is needed\n\nWithout constraints, recommender systems often:\n\n* Favor **popular items** and suppress niche or new ones\n* Treat user groups unequally (e.g., gender, age, region)\n* Under-recommend content from minority creators\n\nThis happens because the data itself is biased.\n\n---\n\n### Common types of fairness\n\n**User-side fairness**\n\n* Different user groups should receive similar quality recommendations\n  (e.g., male vs female users)\n\n**Item-side fairness**\n\n* Items or creators should get fair exposure\n  (e.g., small sellers vs big brands)\n\n**Group fairness**\n\n* Defined demographic groups should not be disadvantaged\n\n---\n\n### How fairness constraints are applied\n\nThey are added **during training or ranking**, for example:\n\n* Limit exposure gaps:\n\n  ```\n  Exposure(group A) ≈ Exposure(group B)\n  ```\n* Penalize unfair predictions:\n\n  ```\n  Loss = Recommendation Loss + λ × Fairness Penalty\n  ```\n\nSo the model trades a *little* accuracy for *much* better equity.\n\n---\n\n### Example\n\n🎬 Movie recommender\nWithout fairness: mostly blockbuster movies\nWith fairness constraint: blockbusters + indie films + diverse creators\n\n---\n\n## 2️⃣ Graph Contrastive Learning in Recommendation Systems\n\n![Image](https://www.mdpi.com/mathematics/mathematics-12-01107/article_deploy/html/images/mathematics-12-01107-g001.png)\n\n![Image](https://moonlight-paper-snapshot.s3.ap-northeast-2.amazonaws.com/arxiv/hgcl-hierarchical-graph-contrastive-learning-for-user-item-recommendation-3.png)\n\n![Image](https://plainer.dev/assets/img/posts/graph-contrastive-learning/GraphCL.png)\n\n### What it means (intuition first)\n\n**Graph contrastive learning** helps a recommender learn **better representations** of users and items by:\n\n* Viewing the data as a **graph**\n* Teaching the model what should be **similar** and **different**\n\nIt’s like saying:\n\n> “These users/items are related — learn that.\n> These ones are unrelated — don’t confuse them.”\n\n---\n\n### Why graphs?\n\nMost recommender systems naturally form graphs:\n\n* Users ↔ Items (clicks, purchases, ratings)\n* Social connections\n* Knowledge graphs\n\nGraph structure = powerful signal.\n\n---\n\n### What “contrastive” means\n\nThe model learns by **comparison**:\n\n* **Positive pairs**\n  Same user/item under different graph views\n* **Negative pairs**\n  Different users/items\n\nThe goal:\n\n```\nMake similar nodes close in embedding space\nPush dissimilar nodes far apart\n```\n\n---\n\n### How it works (simplified)\n\n1. Create two *perturbed views* of the same graph\n   (drop edges, mask nodes, random walks)\n2. Encode both with a graph neural network\n3. Apply contrastive loss:\n\n   ```\n   Pull positives together\n   Push negatives apart\n   ```\n\nThis improves robustness and generalization — especially with sparse data.\n\n---\n\n### Why it’s powerful for recommendation\n\n* Works well with **implicit feedback**\n* Handles **cold-start** better\n* Reduces noise and overfitting\n* Learns structure-aware embeddings\n\n---\n\n## 🔗 How They Fit Together\n\n| Aspect     | Fairness Constraint                 | Graph Contrastive Learning |\n| ---------- | ----------------------------------- | -------------------------- |\n| Goal       | Ethical & equitable recommendations | Better representations     |\n| Focus      | Bias mitigation                     | Self-supervised learning   |\n| Applied at | Objective / ranking                 | Representation learning    |\n| Benefit    | Social responsibility               | Accuracy & robustness      |\n\n👉 Modern systems often **combine both**:\n\n* Graph contrastive learning for strong embeddings\n* Fairness constraints to ensure responsible outcomes\n\n---\n\n## TL;DR\n\n* **Fairness constraints** = rules to prevent biased or unequal recommendations\n* **Graph contrastive learning** = a self-supervised way to learn powerful user/item embeddings from graph data\n* Together, they help build recommenders that are **both smart and fair**\n\n\n",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":120,
			"y":-560,
			"width":360,
			"height":340
		},
