			"text":"# Addiction Aware Content Recommendation",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":320,
			"y":-80,
			"width":360,
			"height":340
		},
		{
			"id":"e695acfa54c3f9fa",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# Sensitive Content Aware Recommendation",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":720,
			"y":-80,
			"width":360,
			"height":340
		},
		{
			"id":"0d3e002ede403225",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# System 1 & System 2\n\nIn recommendation systems, **System 1** and **System 2** are borrowed metaphors from cognitive science (fast vs. slow thinking). They describe **two layers of decision-making** that work together to produce good recommendations.\n\n---\n\n## System 1: Fast, automatic, scalable\n\n**What it does**\n\n* Generates **quick, rough candidate recommendations**\n* Optimized for **speed and scale**\n* Runs on *everyone*, *all the time*\n\n**Typical tasks**\n\n* Retrieve 100–1,000 candidate items from millions\n* Use simple or lightweight models\n\n**Common techniques**\n\n* Collaborative filtering (matrix factorization)\n* Embeddings + nearest-neighbor search\n* Popularity / trending items\n* Simple heuristics (“users like you watched…”)\n\n**Strengths**\n\n* Extremely fast\n* Cheap to compute\n* Works well at large scale\n\n**Limitations**\n\n* Not very precise\n* Ignores subtle context\n* Can be biased toward popularity\n\n👉 Think of System 1 as *“What might this user like?”*\n\n---\n\n## System 2: Slow, deliberate, precise\n\n**What it does**\n\n* **Refines, ranks, and re-orders** System 1’s candidates\n* Optimized for **accuracy and personalization**\n* Runs on a **small candidate set**\n\n**Typical tasks**\n\n* Rank top 10–50 items\n* Apply complex business rules\n* Personalize based on context (time, device, intent)\n\n**Common techniques**\n\n* Deep learning models\n* Learning-to-rank algorithms\n* Context-aware models\n* Multi-objective optimization (relevance, diversity, fairness)\n\n**Strengths**\n\n* High precision\n* Rich understanding of user intent\n* Can balance multiple goals\n\n**Limitations**\n\n* Slower\n* More expensive\n* Not feasible at full scale\n\n👉 Think of System 2 as *“Which of these is best right now?”*\n\n---\n\n## How they work together (real systems)\n\nMost production recommenders are **two-stage (or multi-stage)**:\n\n1. **System 1** → Candidate generation\n   *Millions → hundreds*\n2. **System 2** → Ranking & filtering\n   *Hundreds → final list*\n\nExample:\n\n* Netflix:\n  System 1 finds shows you might like\n  System 2 ranks them based on mood, time of day, recent behavior\n* Amazon:\n  System 1 pulls related products\n  System 2 optimizes for purchase likelihood, margin, diversity\n\n---\n\n## Quick comparison\n\n| Aspect   | System 1             | System 2           |\n| -------- | -------------------- | ------------------ |\n| Speed    | Very fast            | Slower             |\n| Scale    | Massive              | Limited            |\n| Accuracy | Coarse               | High               |\n| Models   | Simple               | Complex            |\n| Role     | Candidate generation | Ranking & decision |\n",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-680,
			"y":-560,
			"width":360,
			"height":340
		},
		{
			"id":"7f0c93e3e14dfd9c",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Here’s a clear breakdown of the **limitations discussed in the paper**, focusing on what the authors themselves flag as potential weaknesses or open challenges. I’ll keep it conceptual and grounded in the actual text from **Section 6: Limitations**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Strong assumptions about user behavior (System-1 vs System-2)\n\nThe entire framework relies on a **dual-system model of human decision-making**, where:\n\n- **System-1** = impulsive, short-term, engagement-driven behavior\n    \n- **System-2** = deliberate, long-term, utility-driven behavior\n    \n\nThe authors acknowledge that:\n\n- This model has **not been validated in real-world recommender deployments**\n    \n- Real users may **keep returning even when their long-term goals are not met**\n    \n- The separation between System-1 and System-2 may not be as clean as assumed\n    \n\nSo, while the model is _inspired_ by psychology and supported by empirical studies, it is still an **approximation**, not a proven behavioral law. The hope is that it provides **directionally better signals** of utility than engagement, even if it’s imperfect .\n\n---\n\n## 2. Return probability is only a proxy for true utility\n\nA key design choice is using **long-term return probability** as a stand-in for user utility. This introduces a limitation:\n\n- Returning to a platform does **not always imply high utility**\n    \n- Users may return out of habit, addiction, social pressure, or lack of alternatives\n    \n- The model explicitly **does not handle long-term addictive behavior**\n    \n\nSo while return behavior may correlate with utility _on average_, it is not a guaranteed or causal measure of user well-being or satisfaction .\n\n---\n\n## 3. Assumption of (approximate) stationarity in user behavior\n\nThe model largely assumes that user behavior and arrival dynamics are **stationary**, but in reality:\n\n- User activity varies by **time of day, season, and life events**\n    \n- Recommendation policies themselves change over time\n    \n- User preferences and utility evolve\n    \n\nThe authors admit that **non-stationarity can confound inference**, especially if changes happen faster than the model can adapt. While some fixes are possible (e.g., time-dependent base intensity μ(t)), these extensions are **not implemented or analyzed** in this paper .\n\n---\n\n## 4. Item embeddings are assumed to be known\n\nAnother simplifying assumption is that **item embeddings are fixed and known**:\n\n- This is justified by the availability of rich item metadata and engagement data\n    \n- However, in practice, item and user embeddings are often **learned jointly**\n    \n\nIf item embeddings are also unknown, then:\n\n- Joint identifiability becomes much harder\n    \n- The theoretical guarantees no longer directly apply\n    \n\nThe authors explicitly note that **joint learning from return behavior alone is an open and challenging problem** .\n\n---\n\n## 5. Synthetic experiments only (no real-world validation)\n\nAll experimental results are based on **synthetic data generated from the same model** used for inference:\n\n- This demonstrates internal consistency and identifiability\n    \n- But it does **not guarantee real-world performance**\n    \n\nThere is no empirical evaluation on real recommender logs, so practical issues like noise, confounding factors, delayed feedback, and partial observability remain untested.\n\n---\n\n## Big picture takeaway\n\nIn short, the paper’s limitations are not about mathematical flaws, but about **model realism and deployment assumptions**:\n\n- Behavioral assumptions may be only approximately true\n    \n- Return probability is an imperfect proxy for utility\n    \n- Non-stationarity and changing policies complicate inference\n    \n- Known item embeddings simplify theory but limit applicability\n    \n- Results are validated synthetically, not empirically\n    \n\nStill, the authors argue that **even an approximate model can outperform engagement-based optimization**, which is the core motivation of the work .\n\nIf you want, I can also:\n\n- Rephrase these limitations for a **paper review**\n    \n- Map them to **future work directions**\n    \n- Or help you critique whether these assumptions are reasonable in a production recommender system",
